This lemma is related to Lemma 51 from \cite{perkovic16}. Our lemma additionally contains the result that if $\langle C_l,C,C_r angle$ is a subpath of $\pstar$, then there is no edge $\langle C_l,C_r angle $ in $\g$. The proofs of this lemma and Lemma 51 from \cite{perkovic16} overlap for cases ef{l:11}-ef{l:13} and then diverge after that. Path $\pstar$ is proper and b-non-causal (ef{l:ncp:pdag} in Lemma~ef{lemma:non-causal-path-pdag}) in $\g$, so it is only left to prove that it is of definite status and that for any subpath $C_l ightarrow C \leftarrow C_r$ of $\pstar$ there is no edge $\langle C_l,C_rangle$ in $\g$. Let $\pstar = \langle X = V_{0}, V_{1},\dots ,V_{k} = Y angle, k > 1, X \in \mathbf{X}, Y \in \mathbf{Y}$. We first prove that $\pstar$ is of definite status, by contradiction. Hence, suppose that a node on $\pstar$ is not of definite status. Let $V_i, i\ge 1,$ be the node closest to $X$ on $\pstar$ that is not of definite status. Then $\langle V_{i-1}, V_{i}, V_{i+1} angle$ is shielded in $\g$ and there is an edge between $V_{i-1}$ and $V_{i+1}$ in $\g$. Let $q = p(X,V_{i-1}) \oplus \langle V_{i-1} , V_{i+1} angle \oplus p(V_{i+1},Y)$ in $\g[D]$. Let $\pstar[q]$ be the path corresponding to $q$ in $\g$. Then $\pstar[q]$ is proper and b-non-causal (Lemma~ef{lemma:non-causal-path-pdag}) in $\g$. Hence, $q$ is also a proper non-causal path (Lemma~ef{lemma:posdir-path}). Since $p$ is a shortest proper b-non-causal path from $X$ to $Y$ that is d-connecting given $\mathbf{Z}$, it follows that $q$ must be blocked by $\mathbf{Z}$. The collider/non-collider status of all nodes, except possibly $V_{i-1}$ and $V_{i+1}$, is the same on $p$ and $q$. Hence, $V_{i-1}$ or $V_{i+1}$ block $q$, so $V_{i-1} 
eq X$ or $V_{i+1} 
eq Y$. We now discuss the different cases for the collider/non-collider status of $V_{i-1}$ and $V_{i+1}$ on $p$ and $q$ and derive a contradiction in each case.  egin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)] \item $V_{i-1}$ is a non-collider on $p$, a collider on $q$ and $\mathrm{De}(V_{i-1},\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} = \emptyset$. Then $V_{i+1} ightarrow V_{i-1} ightarrow V_{i}$ and rule $R2$ implies that $V_{i} \leftarrow V_{i+1}$ is in $\g[D]$. Since $p$ is d-connecting given $\mathbf{Z}$, $\mathrm{De}(V_{i},\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} 
eq \emptyset$. As $\mathrm{De}(V_{i},\g[D]) \subseteq \mathrm{De}(V_{i-1},\g[D])$, this contradicts $\mathrm{De}(V_{k-1},\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} = \emptyset$. \item $V_{i+1}$ is a non-collider on $p$, a collider on $q$ and $\mathrm{De}(V_{i+1},\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} = \emptyset$. This case is symmetric to case ef{l:11} and the same argument leads to a contradiction. \item $V_{i-1}$ is a collider on $p$, a non-collider on $q$ and $V_{i-1} \in \mathbf{Z}$. Since $V_{i-1}$ is of definite status on $\pstar$ it follows that $V_{i-2} ightarrow V_{i-1} \leftarrow V_{i}$ is in $\g$. This implies $V_{i}$ is a definite non-collider on $\pstar$, which is a contradiction. \item $V_{i+1}$ is a collider on $p$, a non-collider on $q$ and $V_{i+1} \in \mathbf{Z}$. Then $V_{i} ightarrow V_{i+1} \leftarrow V_{i+2}$ and $V_{i-1} \leftarrow V_{i+1}$ is in $\g[D]$. $V_{i+1}$ is not of definite status on $\pstar$, otherwise $V_{i}$ would be of definite status on $\pstar$. Thus, there is an edge $\langle V_{i},V_{i+2} angle$ in $\g$. The path $r = p(X,V_{i}) \oplus \langle V_{i} , V_{i+2} angle \oplus p(V_{i+2},Y)$ is proper, b-non-causal (Lemma~ef{lemma:non-causal-path-pdag}) and shorter than $p$. Hence, $r$ must be blocked by $\mathbf{Z}$ in $\g[D]$. The collider/non-collider status of all nodes except possibly $V_{i}$ and $V_{i+2}$ is the same on $r$ and $p$, so either $V_{i}$ or $V_{i+2}$ must block $r$. Since $V_{i} ightarrow V_{i+1} ightarrow V_{i-1}$ is in $\g$, rule $R2$ implies that $V_{i-1} \leftarrow V_{i}$ is in $\g$. Since $V_{i-1} \leftarrow V_{i}$ is on both $r$ and $p$, $V_{i}$ cannot block $r$. Additionally, $V_{i+1} \leftarrow V_{i+2}$ is in $\g[D]$ so $V_{i+2}$ is a non-collider on $p$. Thus, $V_{i+2}$ must be a collider on $r$ and $\mathrm{De}(V_{i+2},\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} = \emptyset$. However, by assumption in ef{l:14}, $V_{i+1} \in \mathbf{Z}$ and $V_{i+1} \in \mathrm{De}(V_{i+2},\g[D])$. \end{enumerate} Lastly, let $C_l ightarrow C \leftarrow C_r$ be a subpath of $\pstar$ and suppose for a contradiction that there is an edge $\langle C_l,C_rangle$ in $\g$. Let $\pstar[q] = \pstar(X,C_l)\oplus \langle C_l,C_rangle \oplus \pstar(C_r,Y)$. Then $\pstar[q]$ is proper and b-non-causal ( Lemma~ef{lemma:non-causal-path-pdag}) in $\g[D]$. Let $q$ in $\g[D]$ be the path corresponding to $\pstar[q]$ in $\g$. Then $q$ is a proper non-causal path from $X$ to $Y$ that is shorter than $p$, so $q$ must be blocked by $\mathbf{Z}$. Then as above, either $C_l$ or $C_r$ must block $q$. Since $C_l$ ($C_r$) is a non-collider on $p$, it must be a collider on $q$ and $\mathrm{De}(C_l,\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} 
eq \emptyset$ ($\mathrm{De}(C_r,\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} 
eq \emptyset$). Since $p$ is d-connecting given $\mathbf{Z}$, $\mathrm{De}(C,\g[D]) \cap \mathbf{Z} 
